class Boolean3(System.Object):
    """Where two bodies lie against each other, face to face.

    The region two convex bodies share, found by clipping one by the faces of the other.

    Booleans of bodies that close only within the tolerance, worked out again on the bodies welded where they are
    not worked out as they came.

    Combines solids: the union of two bodies, the part they share, and one taken out of the other.

    The method is the same one Splition3 uses to cut a plate against a body, carried up a dimension. One body is
    divided by the planes of the other's faces that come near it, which leaves cells that are each wholly inside
    or wholly outside the other body, since the surface of a body never leaves the planes of its own faces. The
    cells wanted for the operation are then glued back together: a face shared by two kept cells appears twice,
    once each way round, and dropping both leaves exactly the outer skin.

    Dividing A by the planes of B already lays a face along every part of the surface of B that runs through A.
    That is why an intersection is just the cells of A that fall inside B, and a difference just the cells of A
    that fall outside it: the walls of the cavity are already there, and adding the faces of B on top of them
    would describe the same surface twice.

    So every operation cuts one body only, the one fewer planes cut, and never by its own faces: they bound it
    already, and cutting by them is what took a bent bar apart into thousands of cells, since every plane of a
    bend runs on through the rest of the bar. A union keeps the part of the cut body beyond the other and the
    other whole; a difference cutting the body taken away keeps the body taken from whole, and the part of the
    other within it turned inside out. Where the whole body and the cells meet, their faces lie back to back in
    one plane, cut differently, and cancel by the area they share. Where a plane crosses a cell and leaves it
    whole, the other body is cut instead, and failing that both are cut by every plane of both, as they once
    always were.

    Every operation reports false when the answer is nothing at all — two bodies that do not touch have no shared
    part, and a body wholly swallowed by another leaves nothing behind. That is an outcome rather than a failure,
    which is why it comes back as false with no result rather than as an exception or an empty body.

    An opening on either body is honoured. A union or a difference cuts the openings into both bodies first
    (Boolean3.TryCutOpenings), so their faces are where their material ends; an intersection adds the planes of
    its own openings near the other to its knives, so no cell straddles a wall, and drops the cells filling them
    as not being material. Either way the result carries the cavity as real geometry rather than as an opening of
    its own. Two bodies too far apart to meet are the exception — nothing is cut there, and each keeps the
    openings it came with. An opening of either body of a common part, or of the body a difference takes away,
    that shares no space with the other body is left out, since it changes nothing the operation gives: its box
    further from the other's box than the tolerance, or it and the other closed, their faces further apart than
    twice the tolerance and no corner of either inside the other. A spiral girder less a spiral ramp holding a
    spiral tube as an opening, 80 under the girder's top all along it, was cut into 640 222 cells in 202 seconds
    and not worked out, the tube cut into the ramp; the tube left out, it is cut into 18 345 in 1.6 seconds and
    holds what slicing gives. The openings of a body a union or a difference keeps material of are cut in as
    before, wherever they lie. Walls of two openings of a body, or of an opening and the body, standing apart
    within the tolerance of one plane are each kept in their own: the openings of both bodies are cut in, and the
    operation worked out, within a finer tolerance, as Boolean3.TryCutOpenings cuts such openings, where no wall
    of the other body lies within the tolerance of the plane of a wall of the one. An L 100 by 80 by 50 with two
    blind openings whose walls stood 0.0009 apart, less a box across both walls, within a thousandth, held 130
    189.39 one way round and 130 189.26 the other, where it holds 130 189.32, as it does now. Asked at a
    millionth or below, where the openings of a body cut in at once come out open, the operation is worked out
    again on the material Boolean3.TryCutOpenings gives, and that is taken where what came as before is not made,
    not valid, or holds another volume: a plate 200 by 100 by 3 with two blind openings whose ceilings stood
    0.00095 apart, less a box across both, at a millionth, held 1.05 too little and was not valid, and the two
    joined were not worked out. Asked below a thousandth, within a million of the origin, openings that come out
    open or with a piece judged whole, cut in at once by the cells each opening reaches, are cut again with every
    plane of the openings carried through every cell, so that the faces of cells side by side match: the plate
    less the box at a ten-thousandth and a hundred-thousandth, where the operation is not worked out again, held
    the same 1.05 too little and was not valid, and the two joined held as much too little or were not worked
    out; both hold what they should now.

    A result of two closed bodies is closed as GeoSolid3.Validate reads it, or not made: one whose corners close
    it, two copies of an edge about the tolerance apart taken for one, which Validate reads as open, is welded,
    or worked out another way, and where it stays open the operation reports false, logged, as one it could not
    work out.

    A void, a chip or a slab no thicker than the point tolerance is within the tolerance of nothing and changes
    nothing; one thicker than the tolerance and a trillionth of the size of the coordinates of the two bodies is
    cut however thin: a cube of 100 less a box 40 by 30 and 0.0005 thick inside it is the cube, and less one
    0.0015 thick holds 999 998.2. One exactly the tolerance thick, on a turned body that thick to a few bits
    either way, is read as no thicker, the same way by the common part and both differences; a union of the two
    can hold anything from the two less it to the two.

    Asked within a few roundings of the coordinates, a quadrillionth of the largest of them, an operation reads a
    corner as on a plane within what rounding adds to the planar tolerance, a trillionth of the size of the
    coordinates, as the cut of openings does: a plate 200 by 100 by 3, turned and a hundred thousand out, less a
    slot 20 wide across all of it, within 9.5E-11, leaves its two pieces, 54 000, where it was said to leave
    nothing. Asked further below the rounding, below four tenths of it, nought among it, an operation is worked
    out as though asked at the rounding, and what it gives is welded to close within the tolerance asked: at
    nought, such a plate at the origin less a hole 20 by 20 through it leaves 58 800, where it came back whole,
    60 000. Of a body that does not close within the rounding, what is given there is taken where it holds up
    against the common part of the two, and otherwise what the tolerance alone gives, where that holds up by the
    same checks; neither holding up, the operation is not worked out: at nought a cube 10 across cracked 0.0001
    wide along three of its faces, less a box turned inside it, is not worked out, where it came back whole, and
    less a slab thousands long through it, sharing 249.85, is not worked out either, where it came back whole
    though the tolerance alone gave something too. The common part of two such bodies is taken only where it has
    no corner outside either, and leaves out no corner of one lying inside the other, and a difference or a union
    of them only where the common part they are checked by does so too: a U 30 by 10 by 10 and a box through its
    notch, both cracked 1.5E-5 wide, have their common part not worked out, where it came out 3.66 of the 34.02
    they share. A difference or a common part said to be nothing is checked so too: such a U less a box dipping
    0.81 into its notch's floor is not worked out, where it was said to leave nothing of 2 402.34.

    A union of two bodies lying face to face within the tolerance of each other and apart, welded closed, is
    taken where it holds the two and the gap filled: two cubes of 100 side by side 0.01 apart, turned, joined
    within a hundredth, hold 2 000 000. What two bodies exactly the tolerance apart join into still depends on
    where they lie: turned another way the two cubes hold 1 999 933.3, part of the gap.

    Two bodies exactly the tolerance into or apart, or nearer, are read as touching, closed across the slab or
    the gap: the part they share is nothing, each difference leaves its body whole, and the union holds the two
    less the overlap or the two and the gap filled, within the tolerance times the area where they lie face to
    face either way, each face within the tolerance of where it belongs. A column 300 by 300 by 1 000 and a beam
    386.7 wide and 364.8 high running exactly 0.001 into its side, turned, joined within a thousandth, hold the
    two less 125.26, the overlap being 109.45 and the beam's whole end 141.07. A beam 160.1 by 160.8 standing
    exactly 0.01 clear of such a column, joined within a hundredth, holds the two and 214.5, filling the gap
    holding 257.4; closed at the default tolerance by a weld tilting the column's face by the tolerance, it held
    661 more than the two and the gap.

    Booleans of bodies standing far out from the origin, worked out near it.

    Combining two flat shapes that lie in one plane in space.

    Cutting a body's openings into it, so that its faces are the surface of its material.

    Booleans of solids worked out with SolidBooleanOptions: the second body's faces lying against the first's put
    onto them, and a result that is not valid worked out again within the fallback tolerance.

    The separate pieces a body is made of, and one body per region two bodies share."""
